{"type": "answer", "run_id": "retrieval-part2-lean-20261110", "scenario": "eval:retrieval", "source": "real", "setting": "small_lean", "setting_def": {"piece_chars": 600, "k": 4, "rerank": false, "router": false}, "drafter": "openai/gpt-oss-120b", "qid": "Q10", "request_id": "retrieval-part2-lean-20261110-small_lean-gpt-oss-120b-Q10", "start_time": "2026-11-10T18:26:35.382+00:00", "degraded": false, "reply": {"request_id": "retrieval-part2-lean-20261110-small_lean-gpt-oss-120b-Q10", "question": "What is Inner Monologue, and how does it give streaming ASR and TTS?", "kind": "answer", "level": 0, "text": "Inner Monologue is a modeling approach that treats speech and text as interchangeable token streams, allowing the same foundation model to be used for different modalities by simply adjusting the temporal relationship between those token types [S1][S2]. By inserting a fixed delay between text and audio tokens, the model can be repurposed as a streaming system: delaying audio tokens by a few seconds gives the model look‑ahead on the text, enabling streaming text‑to‑speech when the text tokens are teacher‑forced at inference [S3]. Conversely, delaying text tokens by a similar amount lets the model listen to incoming audio before emitting text, yielding a streaming automatic‑speech‑recognition system when the audio tokens are teacher‑forced at inference [S3]. This simple delay‑based reconfiguration lets Moshi derive both streaming TTS and streaming ASR without changing the underlying architecture [S2].", "sources": [{"sid": "S1", "cite": "2410.00037v1 p.31 §5.7", "pid": "moshi:600:0184", "paper": "moshi", "block_ids": ["moshi-p31-b005"]}, {"sid": "S2", "cite": "2410.00037v1 p.5 §2", "pid": "moshi:600:0031", "paper": "moshi", "block_ids": ["moshi-p05-b001"]}, {"sid": "S3", "cite": "2410.00037v1 p.31 §5.7", "pid": "moshi:600:0181", "paper": "moshi", "block_ids": ["moshi-p31-b003"]}, {"sid": "S4", "cite": "2410.00037v1 p.18 §4.3", "pid": "moshi:600:0105", "paper": "moshi",